David Razler wrote:
> 
> OK folks, I have a pair of first DEC desktop box tape cartridge
> drives, I believe DX-50, in designation. They have the same mounting
> plate as the RX-50 dual floppy drive.
> 
> 1 - anyone know what kind of cartridge they take?

I'm not familiar with the "DX50".  In DEC terminology, subsystems
that begin with "DX" were traditionally IBM-mainframe channel
connection hardware, so I think it's unlikely that a tape drive would
have that designation.

It seems more likely that you've got a TK50 drive - have you ruled
this out yet?  These (almost) always came attached to DEC skid plates.

It's also possible to have TU58 drives on a skid plate, though this
isn't the most standard way of doing it.

There's also a TK25 drive, taking traditional QIC cartridges.  This
could be coaxed onto a skid plate.

Of course, *any* 5.25" form-factor drive might have been bolted onto
a DEC skid plate by an end-user or third party Q-bus integrator for use
with a non-DEC controller in a BA23 or BA123.

> 2 - anyone know what machines they were used for - they would fit a
> Rainbow (DEC DOS/CPM box) DECMate (PDP-8 box) or Pro-350 (PDP-11 box)

TK50's work on none of the above machines, but of course they do work
in other chassis that hold the skid-plates.  TK25's don't work
with any of the above machines either.  A TU58 could probably be
coaxed onto a Pro-350, but the cabling you describe doesn't sound
close at all, and there'd need to be a serial interface board
in between somehow.

There was also an external tape drive for the DEC Pro that was never
officially released, though there is some OS support for it.  It's
a Cipher QIC drive unit, and (IIRC) it has a QIC-50 (50 pin) interface.
I don't think it was ever used internally to a DEC Pro - after the
RX50's and RD-series hard drive are installed, there's no space left.

> 3 - Owning only a Rainbow and a DECMate II, are there drivers
> available for them? Does it need a special controller card (looks SCSI
> to me)
> 
> 4 - if not, anyone need 'em? (free for shipping/handling)

Tim.



