I don't know if it qualifies as "big", but there are
(or were) third-party 9 track open reel tape drives
that had standard SCSI (the early 50 pin) connections:
or, at least as standard as anything in SCSI-land is.
The only unit I've used myself did not have the big
vacuum columns or glass window: it's a 19" rack mount
unit about a foot high with a front slot to feed in
the tape.

I've used this on a couple of systems here, and I'm
pretty sure one of them was a VAXstation (either an
early 3100 or a 4000-60).

The only reason I used it was to read some tapes and
archive the data onto something newer (DAT) while
the tapes were still readable and the drive still
operable.  Unless you are setting something up
as a historical demonstration there isn't much
use for 9-track open reel tape anymore, and for
historical accuracy you wouldn't put one of those
big floor-standing units on a VAXstation 4000
anyway.  But if you're looking to read and copy
some old media before it rots, this method is
quite practical and finding one of the tape drives
with SCSI is probably easier and cheaper than
building your own controller.  (That is another thread
that occurred recently in some of the PDP-11 related
news groups, and if you want to see why few people
ever design more than one tape controller you can
probably still find it in the news groups, or use
a news archive service like DejaNews.)


