In article <Pine.LNX.4.21.0005212253270.5675-100000@sleepy.lobo.net>, Phillip Williams <phdevax@sleepy.lobo.net> writes:
:I would like to dial up my old VAX which has a DHV11. I have a 
:9600 baud modem connected to one of the ports on the DHV11. Each
:time I try to dail in it comes back with the error Your VAX/VMS
:modem is wired wrong please see your system manager.

  Chances are, your wiring is wrong.

:I thought all modern modems where AT&t compatible or am I missing
:something.

  Start with:

http://www.openvms.digital.com:8000/72final/6136/6136pro_016.html#modemc

  I typically prefer to use "limited" modem control, and not the "full"
  modem control.

  Set the OpenVMS host serial port as SET TERMINAL/MODEM/PERMANENT in
  SYSTARTUP*.COM.  And /AUTOBAUD, if appropriate.

  I'd normally suggest the DECconnect wiring, but the six-wire BC16E
  cabling and the standard connector wiring was effectively insufficient 
  when working with modems.

  If you are working in this area, you really need a break-out box, as
  it makes this debugging much easier.

