Peter Wade wrote:

> A silly question...

    Not silly at all, really. You'd be surprised how many 9-track drives
are still in service. ;-)

> Is it possible to run such a thing from a VAXStation 4000/60? If

    Oh, Essence, yes! Heck, you could run one on a PeeCee with the right
hardware and software. What you'll need, if your VAXen already has SCSI,
is a 9-track drive with a SCSI interface. Such do exist -- I know, since
I own a few. The ones I've seen most often are made by M4 Data, and are
their model 9914. I've also seen one from HP -- the 88780, which is an
OEM version of the 7980.

    Here's the kicker. SCSI-interface 9-trackers do not usually come
cheap (they can run over $1,000 used, and at least $5,000 new). You
might be better off looking around for an older Pertec-interface drive,
and getting hold of a matching QBus-based controller for such for your
VAXen.

> so, what model numbers (or third party units) do I need to be
> looking out for? Are these things high-maintenance as I imagine
> or do they just keep working year after year?

    They just keep going if they're properly cared for. The older Cipher
880 series front-loaders tended to develop leaky vacuum seals after a
few years, and worn hub pads if they were heavily used, but 9-trackers
are typically pretty durable.

