In article <392937a1@www2.iaol.com>, "Peter Wade" <plw@drspc.demon.co.uk> writes:
:As a geek who grew up just when they were phasing-out the big
:spinny magtape drives on minis, I've always thought that owning one
:would be cool! 

  Owning one would be hot, actually.  The ones you very likely remember
  run seriously hot.  (And you pay for the privilege of having all that	
  hot air heated up for you, too.)

:You know the type - you've seen 'em on countless
:sci-fi films and 11/780 publicity shots.

  The large, hot, and power-hungry TU77 and TU78 class drives typically 
  hooked to a MASSBUS formatter.  For OpenVMS, this means you need a 
  VAX-11/750, VAX-11/780 or similar class VAX -- with a MASSBUS adapter.
  (The MASSBUS cable was often likened to a computer room anaconda.)

  The TA78 and TA79 (and TU79 slave) were STI-class drives that were
  usually hooked to an HSC-series CI-based storage controller.

  There were StorageTek tape drives that hooked to the VAX-11/780 SBI 
  or to the VAX-11/750 CMI.  (The SBI is the backplane for VAX-11/780
  series and the I/O for the VAX 8600 series, while the CMI is the 
  backplane for the VAX-11/750 series.)

  Several vendors (including DIGITAL) offered varipis Unibus, Q-bus 
  and SCSI nine-track tape drives, but these were not typically the 
  "juggernauts" that you remember (and that Hollywood has seemingly
  only recently forgotten).

:Is it possible to run such a thing from a VAXStation 4000/60? 

  Most anything is possible in this racket, with the application of
  sufficient money.  (In this particular case, rather more money would 
  be involved.  A TURBOchannel to MASSBUS adapter widget would be rather
  entertaining -- in an expensive way.  The RH780 was the SBI to MASSBUS 
  adapter widget, the RH750 for the CMI. :-)

:If so, what model numbers (or third party units) do I need to be
:looking out for? Are these things high-maintenance as I imagine
:or do they just keep working year after year?

  Lower-maintenance nine-track magtape devices included the TU80 and 
  TU81 series and the TSV series, as well as the TA81+, and various
  of the (more recent) third-party drives.  These were typically not 
  as physically impressive (or as failure-prone) as the "juggernauts" 
  of old, though watching a TU8x or TA8x class tape drive "maytagging" 
  ("middle vintage") does have a charm all its own.

  Compared to more modern tape devices, all of these larger widgets 
  required rather more power (sometimes rather more POWER), and are 
  relatively high maintenance items, and have exceedingly low storage
  density.  (Capacities of 40 MB and 120 MB was not unusual for a
  nine-track 2400 foot reel at 1600 and 6250 BPI, respectively.)


 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




