In article <8gce4e$1dbu$1@nserve1.acs.ucalgary.ca>,
	patchkov@acs1.acs.ucalgary.ca (Serguei Patchkovskii) writes:
> It is a nice result indeed; however, the fact that GS320 gets less than
> 40% of the peak link bandwidth, and only 60% of the memory bandwidth of
> a DS20 is a little bit disapponting - even if understandable. It is also
> interesting to compare GS20 per-CPU bandwidth with "real" supercomputers
> (see http://www.cs.virginia.edu/stream/top10/Bandwidth.html):
> 
> Machine ID                ncpus COPY/ncpus
> ---------------------------------------
> NEC_SX_5-16A                16   37968
> NEC_SX_4                    32   13587
> Cray_T932_321024-3E         32    9710
> Cray_C90                    16    6594
> Cray_Y-MP                    8    2411
> Hitachi_SR8000               8    2604
> NEC_SX3-44                   1   16941
> Compaq_AlphaServer_GS320    32     612
> Cray_J932                   32     594
> Cray_T94                     1   11341
> 
> The current-generation supercomputers (SX5, T90) have almost two orders
> of magnitude more per-CPU bandwidth than GS320; GS320 does manage to
> beat the "economy-range-super" J90 by this metric - which is, in fact,
> no small feat - but only barely. I also seem to recall that three years
> ago, a four-CPU J90 was selling for about the same as a four-CPU GS80 
> lists now ...
> 

The metric you used in your table (per-CPU bandwith) is not a good metric
to describe the balance between computation power and bandwith. Vector
CPUs like the machines on this list have more than one floating point
unit per CPU, usually 4, 8, 16, etc. The Alpha 21264 (GS320) has 2 floating
point units per CPU. A copy bandwith of 37 GByte per CPU would be a complete
waste of resources for an Alpha 264. I believe a better metric would be
bandwith per floating point unit or absolute bandwith without dividing
by anything. Bandwith per CPU can only be used for weird marketing
purposes (which is not the main topic of this news group).
-- 
