I found it:

> Software from SAS Institute, the standard at SB and throughout the
> pharmaceuticals industry, was the underlying tool used for analyzing
> Avandia's clinical trial data -- the largest such undertaking ever at SB.
> Because SB's SAS software converted so readily to the Sun environment,
> and because the Sun EnterpriseTM 10000, or StarfireTM, server performed 33
> times faster than the DEC VAX cluster previously employed, Avandia reached
> the public well ahead of schedule and ahead of the competition. 

> "When we first developed the Avandia program in 1997, we knew
> its clinical trials would be so big a job, and so important to the
> company, that an alternative to the VAX cluster we'd been using
> for years just had to be found," explains Christine James, associate
> director of computing and information sciences at SB. "We had
> struggled with the VAX cluster on many smaller submissions, and we
> knew its performance would never measure up to Avandia's demands.
> We also knew that whenever we pushed the limits of the VAX
> cluster, performance became unacceptable. The flip side of the coin,
> however, was that we were extremely concerned about converting
> to a new environment and all its unknowns. 
> 
> "The decision on a new platform was absolutely crucial and there was
>  very little time to make it," she continues. "Our first thought was to
> stay with DEC's VMS operating system for safety's sake by migrating to
> their Alpha chip architecture. As we looked into it, however, we
> found that the necessary performance still wasn't there and its
> cost was too great for the benefit we would experience." 



