Minkota Tech. <dan@minkota.com> writes:
> I'm trying to make sense of the CPU card for a Alphaserver 2100 - A500 MP-R.
> The Part Number is B2024-AA and information on the internet says its a 4/275
> CPU Module but the Clock on the board says 550Mhz?
>
> Could someone please shed some light on the board?  Is it a 275Mhz or 550Mhz
> processor?

  275. The clock is divided by two. By the way, the "4/" part of the model
number means it is using an older processor type. A "5/" would be a somewhat
newer processor, but still nowhere near current Alpha chips.

> Is it a true 64 bit processor?

  Yes. As far as I know, all Alphas are. Some may have wider-than-64 memory
buses, etc.

> And how does a Dual processor
> version of this DEC Alpha compare to a Pentium II or III Dual Processor type
> unit.

  That's an apples-and-oranges comparison. Roughly, floating point performance
should be about the same and integer performance would be better on the Pen-
tium II/III - but you're comparing an Alpha model discontinued in mid-1996
with a current Pentium.

  In performace-per-square foot, the Pentium would again win against this
older Alpha.

  But if you look at modern Alpha systems like the DS10/DS20 family, you'll
find that the DS10/DS20 boxes, even at much slower CPU speeds than the Intel
parts, will beat the Intel ones handily at FP and be about the same for
integer performance. This is comparing a 1GHz Intel/AMD processor with a
667MHz Alpha.

  Take a look at: http://www.specbench.org/osg/cpu95/results/ and read the
results for yourself.

	Terry Kennedy             http://www.tmk.com
        terry@tmk.com             Jersey City, NJ USA



