in article 391662BF.A9D3A79D@e-farm.com, Jerry Hudgins at jerry@e-farm.com
on 5/8/00 2:46 AM wrote something like:

> Folks:
> 
> I recently purchased a used graphics adapter for my old Alpha 3000-300X
> workstation.  Got a great deal on it, but no documentation.  It
> identifies itself (when queried by the console ROM) as:
> 
> DEC PMAG-DA V5.3g (PMAGB-EA : PXG+ D=24, Z=24)
> 
> I gather that it's a 24-bit smart frame buffer with Z-buffer memory.

    I'm fairly certain this is not an sfb incarnation. sfb is generally 2D
whereas PixelSTAMP (PX/PXG) and PixelVision (PV/???) fb's was designed more
or less for 3D only.

    I have a PMAG-E (PXG+) that reports itself as

    1: PMAG-DA  DEC     V5.3f   TCF0 (EA: PXG -- D=24)
                no Z-buffer RAM installed

    I'm fairly certain this is the same card you have only a newer firmware
revision. (Which I believe there are one or two flash updates for, though I
do not have them)
    I've never had much luck in Ultrix 4.5 getting any graphics options to
work other than what was installed in the box when Ultrix was installed. I
can't speak for OSF/1 because I don't have it; but I imagine not much has
changed installer wise. If anyone has a copy of Digital Unix prior to v4.0b
(for MIPS DECstation support), any chance of me getting a hold of it?
    The reason X won't run on this card is probably because there is a
separate X server for PX and one for PXG/true color fb's. On Ultrix there
were three different X servers: the sfb,mfb,cfb server, a special X server
for the PXG series and yet another X server for True Color FB's (PMAG-E,
PMAG-F, and I believe PMAG-J/RO). Look for an install package or update with
the True Color X server and that should get you going. In Ultrix it is
called Xwst3D and is it /usr/bin/X11/ IIRC.
    The lack of documentation is a big problem, the entire PixelStamp
project was company confidential information. No documentation was ever
released on it, or the cards themselves. If you find anything please pass it
along!
    Someone on Port pmax or port Alpha mailing lists on
http://www.netbsd.org might be able to provide you with more information
than I have. Hope that helps

    Chris
 
