>Im a young UNIX-freak, who has become a bit interested in the VMS
>operating system :-). So, i want a VAX and i have been offered some used
>VAXen:
>
>Digital VAXstation 3100/M76 - SPX
>Digital VAXstation 4000 - 60
>Digital VAXserver 3100 (Scsi disk Ext. SCSI udtag)
>
>and a Digital Storage Expansion (99 MB Disk + CD-ROM)
>
>Basically, i just want to learn OpenVMS, DECwindows a little bit of
>system administration, and perhaps some programming. 

Great!  Welcome to the club!

> I thought it would
>be a good idea to buy the Storage Expansion as well, so that i can
>install programs and newer releases of OpenVMS on the system.

For a VAX, unless you want to worry about tailoring, what (not) to 
install and so on, you need a 500 MB system disk; 1 GB is better.  The 
system disk can be in an external box.  (This can be an advantage, as it 
is easier to stick it on another system.

>So i ask you guys, (since i know nothing about VAX), which configuration
>would you suggest? The VS4000 or VS3100-M76?

All are nice; personally, I wouldn't recommend anything slower than the 
3100 M76.  The 4000/60 is the better machine---faster and can take an 
arbitrarily large system disk (not that you need it); 3100s are limited 
to 1.07 GB (just system disk, not other disks).  Otherwise, the 3100 M76 
is nice.  Don't know about the VAXserver.

>And finally and very important :-), what should i pay for it?

Nothing.  That is, if you're lucky you can get these sorts of machines 
for free (I even got a 4000/90 and a 4000/100A for free) or these days 
even an older ALPHA for free.

By the way, I was on holiday on Bornholm last summer---nice place!




