Alan.J.Thackray <alant@aethos.co.uk> writes:
> Is there a  difference between my QLA-1040, and a genuine DEC KZPBA ??

  Quite possibly. It appears as though the old DEC money-grab of charging
SCSI controller makers who wanted to include MSCP (which was all of them)
has reappeared in some form on the PCI adapter cards.

  IntraServer (who makes the KZPCM-DA) charge much, much more for versions
of their cards that work under VMS. Their list price for the board is 
$795 (which is better than Digipaq's $1095. But the same card for Windows
NT/Alpha or Linux/Alpha is $558. Even their low-end single-port Ultra Wide
card has this price difference - $315 for VMS, $175 for NT/Alpha.

  In the case of the IntraServer/Digipaq KZPCM-DA, if you try using the
generic card, VMS will begin to boot but then the SCSI port driver will
complain about an invalid checksum. This even happens if you take a real
DEC KZPCM-DA and upgrade the firmware (since both Digipaq and IntraServer
ship their cards with an incredibly ancient SDMS BIOS - 4.08 while the
current one is 4.16).

  There's a separate (soldered-on) flash chip on these boards, which is
apparently needed for this license check.

