Mr. Lee:

The obvious answer is to replace the battery.  However, it is a specialized
battery (4.5v), and probably not readily available in Korea.  Am I correct
to assume that is where this note originates from?
A simple diagram of the power plug and a possible solution is as follows:
o   o   x   o
   |   |       |
   |   |       +------ +4.5 Volts
   |   |
   |   +-------------- not connected (pin is just for polarizing the plug)
   |
   +------------------ GND
(NB: the pin marked x is a missing pin, the plug has a blocker to prevent
reverse polarity insertion). The battery allows the CMOS memory to keep
bootstrap
settings and the date while the power is off.
I used three ``energiser'' AA cells and they have worked fine so far.

I have read of ways to boot the Multia without a battery, however I
understand that it causes reliability problems.

Good luck!!

