Tim Llewellyn <tim.llewellyn@bbc.co.uk> writes:
> You mean make VMS as unreliable as unix?

  Define "unreliable" - most of my Unix boxes have been up for 216 days
(since the last software upgrade) and some that don't get upgraded any
more have been up for 523 days. And these are running on PC hardware.

> Ok, so you slap a UPS on the
> box so it doesn't crash due to power glitch. Then its just OS panics, sysadmins
> with sticky fingers or an oversubscribed system randomly killing processes
> you have to watch out for. Any one of those will crap on your data that you
> expected to be on disk but is actually still in the unix system buffers.

  A couple points:

  1) If you assert that VMS is more reliable than Unix, then if a Unix panic
     is acceptable to the application, a bugcheck on VMS (which should hap-
     pen less often) is also acceptable to the application, and the system is
     still more reliable.

  2) disk caching has to be an OS-level interface, not per-process, so killing
     a process won't cause previously-cached unwritten data to be lost, though
     it may leave the file in an inconsistent state if the app was in the pro-
     cess of updating it. And if you have operators killing production jobs,
     you've got other problems.

