David made several good points, but it should also be noted that VMS does in
fact support various forms of write-back caching - just not in any way that
anyone save a knowledgeable VMS user is likely to use.  There may be
marginal advantages to centralizing the write-back mechanism in system
memory (rather than replicating write buffers in each application's virtual
memory), but there are also advantages to avoiding the memory-to-memory copy
that centralizing the mechanism requires (since it's reasonable to assume
that the vast majority of Unix applications that benefit from write-back
caching do not need to be able to share non-persistent updates with other
processes - and the percentage of Unix applications that use 'direct I/O'
and private buffers to avoid this overhead is likely even lower than the
percentage of VMS applications that use the write-back facilities VMS
provides).

Aside from dating from a period when more than a few MB of physical memory
on a multi-user system was unusual, VMS also takes a conservative approach
to data persistence, while providing mechanisms that allow knowledgeable
users to be more daring when they know it's safe.  Unix, in general, assumes
the application developer knows what s/he's doing, and goes for performance
while providing facilities that allow finer control over persistence by
knowledgeable users.  So in general VMS gets a reputation (among
unknowledgeable users) for being slower, while Unix applications written by
less-than-expert developers get the reputation for being flakey under
failure conditions.

Which approach is better is largely a matter of taste, but the fact that a
large majority of developers seem to prefer the Unix approach strongly
suggests that providing it as an *option* to applications on VMS might make
VMS at least somewhat more attractive to a wider range of developers and the
users who use their applications (or even VMS applications that don't need
the additional protection but weren't coded to avoid its overhead).

- bill

