Paul:

Welcome to the world of REAL computing.
Which Multia do you have?  Look on bottom of the unit, there's a sticker with
the model number on it.  It will read VX4(0,1,2 ?) - (2,3)F
This will tell us which version of Multia it is, and what the processor speed
is.
Does it have a SCSI riser card in it?
Does the memory simms have any markings on them? (memory has to be TRUE parity
70ns or better, matched pairs)
Does it have a battery? If it has a battery, does pin 4 have 4.5V? (Pin three
is blanked out on the plug).  If the battery is dead or even low, it will not
boot.
What kind of Monitor are you using?  Is it a fixed frequency or multi sync?
Which keyboard are you using? An original or a regular PC style with an adapter
plug?
Does it have a floppy drive?
So many questions. Sigh.

The Multia takes over 30 seconds to begin booting.  How long have you waited?
Turn the monitor brightness to it's highest setting.  Do you see a blinking
underscore ( _ ) the the lower left hand corner of the screen?  If so, then
you're halfway there.
If there is a blur of lines across the bottom, the monitor sync rate is not
correct for the Multia.  Depending upon which version of Multia you have, there
are jumpers on the mother board underneath the hard drive bracket.  You may
have to change the setting.  Word of warning, they are not marked.  Here's a
link that will be helpful:
http://www.progsoc.uts.edu.au/~telford/reference/multia/
This should get you started.  Here's a few links that were VERY helpful for me:

http://www.netbsd.org/Ports/alpha/multiafaq.html
http://home.earthlink.net/~djesys/vms/hobbyist/multia.html
http://www.wei.com/multia/qswhatis.html
http://www.dreamtime.org/multia/cpumicromart/multia.html#NT
http://www.brouhaha.com/~eric/computers/udb.html#other
http://www.cse.unsw.edu.au/~s2230937/putn/multia/

There's more, but this will get you started.
BTW, I have 3 Multia's on my home net, one running Windoze NT, one running Red
Hat Linux 6.0 and one running OpenVMS 7.1 1H2, they're all networked and are
running as they should.  These are not really fast machines, but so very
versatile!
I've bought another one which I'll be attempting to set up True64 Unix (if
it'll work, not sure yet)

If you need any help, do not hesitate to drop me an eMail.

Tom Crabtree
tccrab@sunset.net
backyard shade tree computer mechanic

