Sorry for the acidity level of my previous response.  I suspect I was
responding to Tim's tone in the included excerpt more than to yours.

And I should have included examples of how to provide safe write-back
caching rather than just asserted their existence.  They include (in
approximate ascending order of preference, IMO) only write-back-caching user
updates (least complexity, least performance boost), 'soft updates' (which
maintain complete write-back dependency graphs in memory and avoid cycle
problems by maintaining information allowing 'roll back' to a previous state
before a write occurs - I think this has appeared in BSD Unix variants, and
my recollection is that Spiralog had a mechanism at least something like
this), and logging techniques (which balance the additional cost of a log
write - that can be batched with other log writes in many cases - against
the ability to write the actual data and meta-data updates back in any order
and at any future time, subject to cycle-avoidance similar to that for soft
updates; this also minimizes exposure to failures without severely limiting
the ability to 'coalesce' frequent updates to the same data over a
significant period of time, since once the log record has been written the
action is persistently recoverable, and speeds synchronous operations, since
only the log record - plus any update too large to be reasonably included in
the log - must be written synchronously to make the action persistent).

There are enough good ways to avoid disk write activity with logging and
write-back caching even for synchronous operation to make log-structured
storage (given its impact on on-disk contiguity) an unattractive
alternative, IMO.  And for operations that do not require up-to-the-second
confirmation of disk writes, it's a clear win that still preserves file
system integrity when implemented correctly.

- bill

