In article <8f40pp$fl$1@kadath.deep.it>, Cthulhu <cthulhu@kadath.deep.it> writes:
>
>> format if the tape was first degaussed with a bulk tape eraser.  They
>
>The bulk eraser is that big magnet? :)
>

I realize you were joking, but just 'a big magnet' isn't always
enough to erase these tape cartriges.  The material used in
this type of tape cartridge is very good at retaining a magnetic
field.  A tape eraser that can completely wipe audio tapes,
video cassettes, and DAT tapes, will hardly touch one of these
if used from the outside.  I did successfully recover some
TK50 / TK70 cartridges with such a consumer bulk eraser, but
only by opening the door on the cartridge and pulling out the
leader so I could put the eraser in direct contact with the
tape.

Anyone remember those stories about people who were planning
to wreak havok with some (government) computer site by running
down the aisle of the tape library with a big permanent magnet
in their hands to 'erase' the tapes?  It won't happen.  A
permanent magnet strong enough to damage these tapes would probably
be too heavey to lift.

>> you could have a bad cartridge.  There used to be a FAQ that listed
>
>They are used cartidges, I don't think they're bad.
>

Sometimes the cartridges become bad through use.  Tapes do
eventually wear out after enough passes over the heads (though
they last longer than they used to).

P.S.: an addendum to my previous post.  The manual I have does
not say why switch 1 is always on.  If it did I would have
passed on the information.

-- 
