In article <nulinnkeep-0705001746570001@192.168.1.11>, nulinnkeep@compuserve.com (Walt Shekrota) writes:
:But I do not know the pinout of lk401 kb or I wouold splice it together.
:It has modular 4 pin jack. (RBYG)

  This is a FAQ -- no offense intended here, but please remember to check 
  the OpenVMS FAQ and other available resources -- the keyboard and mouse 
  pinout is not the interesting part of this "I need an adapter" effort, 
  it's the protocol. 

  The PC keyboard and mouse protocol differs from the old DIGITAL protocol.

  The protocol definition for the old DIGITAL keyboard and mouse interfaces
  is buried at the back of the QDSS section in the old VAXstation II manual, 
  specifically, in the back of the VCB02 Video Subsystem Technical Manual 
  (EK-104AA-TM).  The keyboard wiring and protocol is in appendix B, and
  occupies circa 44 pages.  The mouse is in appendix C, circa 12 pages.

  The usual solution: get an LK201 or LK401 or LK402 series keyboard, and 
  an appropriate member of the VSXXX-?? series mouse.

  On many systems, the system console will try to "ping" the local keyboard
  port, to see if a keyboard is attached.

  The pinout for the DECconnect serial-line MMJ (modified modular jack) is 
  rather easier to connect to than is the keyboard RJ (as you don't need to 
  deal with a protocol conversion), and the DECconnect MMJ pinout is located 
  in the OpenVMS FAQ.

 --------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
   Hoff (Stephen) Hoffman   OpenVMS Engineering   hoffman#xdelta.zko.dec.com




