DFWDAYS: DECUS Does Dallas

The twice-yearly DECUS US Chapter symposium is a thing of the past, but
DECUS members need not wait until October for the CETS2000 symposium in Los
Angeles to get a heaping helping of the latest Compaq Faqs and Red Hot
Product Updates.
The Dallas Fort Worth Compaq Users Group is putting the finishing touches on
the Y2K instantiation of its DFWDAYS regional symposium. Last held two years
ago, the Y2K edition of the DFWCUG regional event promises to eclipse the
success of its popular and well-attended predecessor. Here's a few good
reasons why you should consider spending the first weekend of June deep in
the heart of Texas together with your DECUS and Compaq colleagues.

On Tap In Texas

DFWDAYS, A Compaq Update Weekend, will feature over 80 hours of technical
sessions on topics ranging from Alpha to Zero Latency Enterprise technology.
The agenda includes sessions on Linux, OpenVMS 7.3 Futures, Web-enabling
OpenVMS Applications, Tru64 UNIX and TruCluster V5 Updates, Fib- rechannel
Technology, Windows 2000, Compaq Desktops, Alpha and ProLiant Server
Roadmaps, Storageworks, Compaq Hobbyist Programs, eCommerce,
Internetworking, and much, much more. There's also a special OpenVMS Galaxy
Lab Seminar that'll provide hands-on experience in getting Galactic.

In addition to the regular sessions, three keynote addresses are on tap.
Kicking off the event on Friday morning June 2, OpenVMS VP Rich Marcello
will deliver an update on OpenVMS Strategies and Futures. SKC will provide
Friday evening pre-prandial entertainment by presenting the latest
instantiation of our Compaq's Strategic Directions talk, newly fortified
with lurid details on things WildFire, Alpha, and Himalaya-related. A new
version of SKC's Top Ten Fearless Prognostications is planned as well. And
in his first DECUS presentation in a dog's age, Network-1 Senior Consultant
Dr. Bill Hancock on Saturday evening will discuss Internet Security beyond
Firewalls. Given the rash of viral outbreaks and denial of service attacks
that have characterized the first half of Y2K, the good doctor's
prescriptions should be timely indeed.

Electro-Vlasic technology is "just so twentieth-century," accordingly no
phosphorescent pickles will cast their garish light on the session hall
during this year's DFWDAYS event. Instead, senior researchers from the
DFWCUG Applied Science Directorate will offer a first-ever demonstration of
a new breakthrough in directed energy technology.

Great Bang for the Buck

The admission price for this three-day, event is a mere $195. The fee
includes admission to all sessions and keynotes, daily breakfasts, lunch on
Friday and Saturday lunch, and evening events. DFWDAYS will be held at the
DFW Airport Marriott Hotel, where rooms can be had at a special rate of $79
per night. Do the math on the price/ performance: you can't beat this deal
with a stick!

If the bargain basement price and the promise of good old fashioned Southern
hospitality isn't enough to convince you to line up, sign up, and enlist in
the DWFDAYS event; there's the chance to win Swell Prizes. Being offered as
a door prize is a Sony Model LDI-D50B Glasstron, a professional, 180K-pixel
head-mounted video display that simulates a 52-inch screen display situated
seven feet away from the viewer. The lucky winner of this $1,800
technological tour de force will henceforth be able to "just say no" to
15-inch PC monitors.

Registrations are running way ahead of expectations, so to ensure that the
event managers aren't forced to "just say no" to your reservation, be sure
to visit http://dfwdays.dfwcug.org/ and register online as soon as possible.
Based on our past experience with DFWCUG get-togethers DFWDAYS is an event
you just don't want to miss. SKC sure hopes to see you there!



