In article <8ejt71$e5$1@kadath.deep.it>, Cthulhu <cthulhu@kadath.deep.it> writes:
>
>2) will a linux box be able to use it, with a SCSI2 controller
>(AHA-2940 or NCR810)?
>

There is a reasonable chance (I don't remember if I ever tried
this combination).

>
>5) There is a 4 switch pack on one side. I assume:
>	1-3:	SCSI ID
>	4:	Termination
>   maybe:
>   	1:	Termination
>	2-4:	SCSI ID
>

Holding the drive upright with the tape opening to the left
and the connectors to the right,  the switch pack should
be on the lower right corner.  Switch 1 is at the bottom,
switch 4 is at the top.  The switch pack should also have
the numbers printed on it.

Switch 1 should always be to the left (On).

Switches 2, 3, and 4 select the SCSI ID.  The suggested
setting is unit 5.  Note that many VAXstations that were
built about the same time as this drive set the controller
to unit 6 rather than unit 7, so the manual says not to
use unit 6.   Most newer controllers are set to 7, but
you should check your system.  Other unit settings:

Lun              2      3    4
0               Right Right Right
1               Left  Right Right
2               Right Left  Right
3               Left  Right Right
4               Right Right Left
5               Left  Right Left
6               Right Left  Left
7               Left  Left  Left

The manual says not to use a pencil to move the switches.
Bits of graphite can flake off and get into the switches
and damage them.

The primary value of one of these drives is if you have
to read TK50s from other systems, or if you don't have
another tape mechanism for backup.  TK50 and TK30 drives
don't have the best reputation for standing up to really
heavy duty use: but the media itself is generally quite
reliable.  I've been able to read very old tapes with
no loss of data.  And there are still a lot of these
drives still working.

-- 
