Jerome Fine replies:

I tried to use a TZ30 drive on a PC some time ago, but I also did not have the
necessary host adapter.  The drive I had came with an enclosure that converted
the 50 pin header (normal female type on the drive side) connector PLUS 4 pin
power connector (normal female power type for a 5 1/4" drive) to a 60 pin
female connector.  Because I did not have the corresponding interface on either
the PC (or the PDP-11 where I was actually able to get the drive to work on a
CMD 220/TM host adapter), I removed the enclosure and used the normal
internal 50 pin header and 4 pin power separately.  I  WAS  UNABLE  TO  GET
THE  TZ30  TO  WORK  ON  THE  PC.  I am sure it was because I was using
a host adapter that was NOT  SUITABLE.  When I tried the TK50Z-GA with that
same host adapter on the PC, it would not work there either.

I am told that a SCSI-2 connection consists of a 50 pin header and a separate 4 pin
power connection.  But I have also heard that this is or can be SCSI-1.  Does
anyone else know which is which?

My key question is the purpose for which you want to use the TZ30?  In my
case, I run RT-11 on a PC and want to be able to read tapes from a TK50
on a real PDP-11 that were produced under RT-11 on the real PDP-11.
I have been successful in achieving that goal, but not with a TZ30.  However,
I now believe that I would be successful with a TZ30 as well which I do want
to try if I can get one again for testing.

> > 2) will a linux box be able to use it, with a SCSI2 controller
> > (AHA-2940 or NCR810)?
>   You could hook it up and see if the SCSI BIOS can even see it. If not,
> then you're stuck.

In my case when I first tried the TZ30 and was UNSUCCESSFUL, the BIOS
was able to see the TZ30 on the PC and even able to WRITE to the CompacTape,
but not to READ the CompacTape correctly - a drive/host adapter problem, not
the CompacTape.  I subsequently acquired an Adaptec AHA-2940 as well, but
I have not had a chance to test either the TK50Z-GA or a TZ30.

However, I also acquired an Adaptec AHA-1522 (as Terry Kennedy mentioned
above) and that was successful in using a TK50Z-GA on the PC under RT-11
UNDER the Eratz-11 emulator by John Wilson.  CompacTapes that are
READ/WRITE under RT-11 on a real PDP-11 with a TK50-AA and a TQK50
can be interchanged with CompacTapes that are READ/WRITE using a TK50Z-GA
on a PC under RT-11 (of course UNDER the Eratz-11 emulator by John Wilson).
I presume that the TZ30 will also function correctly in this configuration, but I no longer
have the TZ30 to be tested.

I am hoping that it will be possible to run RT-11 on a Linux box (a PC with Linux
installed as opposed to W95/W98 - yeck!), not an Alpha with Linux installed.
In this case, it will hopefully be possible to run the TK50Z-GA and the TZ30
under RT-11 on a Linux box as well in so-called raw SCSI mode using Eratz-11.
Of course, if you are not running PDP-11 software on your PC (Linux or W98
operating system using Eratz-11), then you will have to supply your own software.

For a PC with Linux, I hope it will be possible to do everything that can presently
be done under DOS/W95/W98 (also WNT but not as much - the hardware is
somewhat more hidden) using Eratz-11 to emulate a real PDP-11, except of
course that with the fastest 1 GHz AMD CPUs (and eventually the Pentium III CPUs
as well), a PC using Eratz-11 can be up to TEN times as fast as a real PDP-11/93.
I don't think a benchmark has been done as yet on the fastest 1 GHz AMD CPU,
but I am confident that the TEN times as fast as a real PDP-11/93 will at least be
close, if not exceeded in some benchmarks.  Note that I have seen TWICE as
fast just with a Pentium 166 MMX.  So TEN times with a 1 GHZ CPU does
not seem unreasonable.

> > 3) why the lock/unlock lever can be turned on "lock" position even if
> > there is no cartidge inside? Hardware fault?
>   I think that's the default behavior. It raises the capstan onto the bottom
> of the drive. The lock (preventing moving the lever) only engages once the
> drive has loaded the tape.

I know that the TK50-AA in a real PDP-11 or in a TK50Z-GA box does
have interlocks which prevent the user from doing some things.  BUT, the
"lever" on the TK50-AA can be moved up and down with or without
a cartridge inside, so this action on the TZ30 is quite similar.

> > 4) Isn't the TZ30 the SCSI version of the TK50? Don't they use the
> > same DECtape cartidges?
>   Mostly. The TK50Z is the SCSI TK50. The TZ30 is a newer drive, which was
> rumored to be the product of a bet between Misubishi and DEC.

Interesting - as they would say on laugh-in!!

> > 5) There is a 4 switch pack on one side. I assume:
> >       1-3:    SCSI ID
> >       4:      Termination
> >    maybe:
> >       1:      Termination
> >       2-4:    SCSI ID
>   I don't remember, but 3 of the switches are the ID.

I also found the 4 switch pack.  Since the CMD 220/TM requires the tape
SCSI  ID to be "4", I did set that value and used the TZ30 a MU0: under
RT-11. In addition, the CMD 220/TM does not seem to be very fussy
about termination, so I don't know if the fourth position is for that purpose.
But, 1-3 do seem to be SCSI  ID since I set the SCSI  ID to be "4" by
turning on switch "3" (I think - if I remember correctly).

> > 7) How am I supposed to manually clean the heads?
>   The same cleaning kit as you use for a TK50 - 22-00436-01, which has (as
> far as I can tell) never been available except through Field Service.

Does anyone have any of these - are they still available - any price estimates?

> > 8) Is there somewere some IR sensor I should check?
