Cthulhu <cthulhu@kadath.deep.it> writes:
> Wasn't the TZ30 commonly used in the MicroVAX 3100, wich were SCSI?
> Or the 3100s were sort-of SCSI too? :)

  The VAXstation/VAXserver 3100's used an NCR-based (810, if I remember
right) controller. Given that with NCR chips, you essentially write the
controller microcode, you can do whatever you want to talk to oddball
devices...

> Oh. But they (TZ30) used TK50s, or DLT tapes (OpenVMS 7.2 SPD list the
> TZ30 in the DLT devices...)?

  TZ30's and TK50's used the TK50K tape, AKA "CompacTape". The TK70 used
the TK70K tape, AKA "CompacTape II". Later DLT drives used different media,
though some could also read TK50's.

