Cthulhu <cthulhu@kadath.deep.it> writes:
>> Doen't remember the TZ30 anymore. But watch. There are many linux kernels
>> out there which have problems with it.
>
> Why?

  Could be a number of reasons (I've never run Linux) but it could be things
like fixed vs. variable block, CCS vs. SCSI-2, or general weirdness in the
drive firmware.

> Was it really possible to format a TK50 as a TK70?
> And if it was, how can I reformat them as TK50?

  If the tapes were completely blank (never used, or bulk-erase by a power-
ful enough eraser) the media would interchange (init a TK50 in a TK70 at
TK70 density, or vice versa). The TK70 was *not* a dual-density device, so
tapes initialized on a TK70 were only readable there. The actual testing of
the media might have been different - Digital certainly said it was.

  Also, you can hook a TK50 drive up to a TK70 controller and it would work
(as a TK50). Field Service used to do this to pacify customers who suffered
from a couple specific TK50 controller bugs that were fixed on the TK70 con-
troller but not on the TK50 controller.

	Terry Kennedy             http://www.tmk.com
