Benjamin Keilty wrote:
> 
> I had this problem once after transporting a DEC 3000/500. I think it
> had to do with the memory or boards that hold it. My fix was making sure
> that the memory and the boards that they are on are plugged in snug.
> After that it came up fine.

There's same code on the 3000-900 can come up if the I/O
board isn't making a good connection with the main board. I think
the memory boards can cause it as well. That's what it was the first
few times it happened to us.

Last time, it needed a new main board (service contract, luckily).

Never seen the inside of a 3000-500 (just the -400, -600, -700 and 
-900), so I don't know if there are any modules besides the memory
cards. I'd just make sure everything's seated correctly.



